#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "7aec717f-6f52-590d-9cd0-47c34b587e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5551" :question/problem "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/16e82b08842bdec1de657e14110b08037cca4452d1f0e8c64a8fd783037adeb6.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/559785dc3071ba5018f9b97d45e18e9f7bf937f702f9eeaf0a9c5ae087d5b43f.png) are orthogonal vectors, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot 6+k\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-20-18-6k-16 \\\\  & =-6k-54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the last line to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6k-54 & =0 \\\\ -6k & =54 \\\\ k & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133501 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "ce2ff5a8-84bb-5d2b-8078-623203f1a3b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5552" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/61cb89adc9a0cce6a434c41be5d2d1799e01e94bebe1b4f44d48bc011c1a349b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/00af13e5017635a0b333ecd5fc2bfc761c9612427b8a3986ba48fa904ff2eed0.png) be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0$. Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w) & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +((-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 4)\\, \\\\  & =(-2-9+4)+(-2+6+20) \\\\  & =-7+24 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "8b0122e7-3500-50cf-8505-22970428dac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5553" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/618da6573504088a90064c503b3603f9c1cbedaee493473db435765ca635bdb9.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/a71f47b53dab435e794fa5c4c889141b8ce32c93715abd56e0dc48d6535865b8.png) be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=0$. Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -8\\cdot 6+a\\cdot (-15)+b\\cdot 9+(-10)\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ -48-15a+9b+120 & =0 \\\\ -15a+9b & =-72 \\\\ -5a+3b & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ -8\\cdot 4+a\\cdot 2+b\\cdot (-5)+(-10)\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ -32+2a-5b+30 & =0 \\\\ 2a-5b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5a+3b=-24 \\\\ 2a-5b=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10a+6b=-48 \\\\ 10a-25b=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, adding the above equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n-19b = -38 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b = 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b = 2$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-5(2) & =2 \\\\ 2a-10 & =2 \\\\ 2a & =12 \\\\ a & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b = 6 + 2 = 8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133553 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "31abdef6-68e0-5c10-b03d-9d835ea6c681" :question/math-academy-id "e-5909" :question/problem "Given the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/b8e1e42d3c85f7b3783a0c46d5075e0c588f22a030f3d6a84da9ad3444ad35d0.png), which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/6e69c8002a518487ce4821efe1faae85ba7ec365b1bedf23d7bfcd30e10e42b5.png)" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-5+9-8+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-10-6+16+0 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot (-8)+0\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-10+12-16+0 \\\\  & =-14.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $\\mathbf{w}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "712cb48a-1e30-5273-b389-6cda4df8b644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323630} {:answer/id #uuid "dd688522-2854-5453-a151-6fb8b1c88bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323631} {:answer/id #uuid "2b505efd-a26e-55f6-9f06-1ce7c9b06ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323632} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7ac459-a35b-5099-aa35-f3d4e8789ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323633} {:answer/id #uuid "94dbccea-843a-504f-8dbb-7ed992c106bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b505efd-a26e-55f6-9f06-1ce7c9b06ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323632} :answer-field/id #uuid "098f2ecc-6d21-5a9e-9d6c-5236648f2c40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6462a8a7-0592-5fe5-81e2-36fc28907977" :question/math-academy-id "q-140123" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3k \\\\ -6 \\\\ k \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that  $u⊥v.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 3k+1\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot k+(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{k}^{2}-6+3k+0 \\\\  & =3({k}^{2}+k-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({k}^{2}+k-2) & =0 \\\\ 3(k+2)(k-1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2805279-538c-5444-9ce2-5b78dfd01067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323637} {:answer/id #uuid "c1918eb1-b3e1-5368-8cff-16767769ca9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323638} {:answer/id #uuid "842ecc25-9557-5c66-a5da-ae25e705113a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323639} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcf8754-1699-5f06-aa53-be265f9827b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323640} {:answer/id #uuid "998d878f-2bf9-5e14-9237-a1d64e50ccf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1918eb1-b3e1-5368-8cff-16767769ca9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323638} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c93119-93b3-5f19-a158-d5f3170fc867" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24f523eb-fc00-5d09-a560-8d4fb04957ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-140125" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,w.$ Find $(w+v)\\cdot (2u+3w).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(w+v)\\cdot (2u+3w) & =2(w\\cdot u)+2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)+3(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0+2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)+3(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =2(v\\cdot u)+3(w\\cdot w)+3(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =2(-4\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3)+(-4)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & +3(1\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & +3(-4\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+2\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2(-40)+3(34)+3(2) \\\\  & =-80+102+6 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc48d389-92f8-591e-98fd-bb73d96edce0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323644} {:answer/id #uuid "19127bdd-b733-5880-878a-596769b4461f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323645} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8d3ad1-372c-5d77-8513-db5f09812807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323646} {:answer/id #uuid "2e12668a-0cec-5437-a009-1f649f37fd7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323647} {:answer/id #uuid "f200254a-7c0a-57f0-84e2-5eb2a7be5224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f200254a-7c0a-57f0-84e2-5eb2a7be5224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323648} :answer-field/id #uuid "50c11be6-72c9-5458-8f76-18c1f15c36ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec29a877-905c-572a-b48b-a0c1a6d38c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39292" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,w.$ Find $(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w) & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4+5\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & +(1\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 5+4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(6-4-5-6)+(3+3+10-8) \\\\  & =-9+8 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b2a881-6b7b-51f8-bce9-ecb43a86bc94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323651} {:answer/id #uuid "fea8cb18-1199-5933-8b61-6f65fd1ef25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323652} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8eec84-0ae2-5f49-b0cd-d7ab80edf0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323653} {:answer/id #uuid "02563a48-9dcd-5ed2-a831-07cbfc10b333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323654} {:answer/id #uuid "51e43284-2d58-512c-8c73-d7463f4f0ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce8eec84-0ae2-5f49-b0cd-d7ab80edf0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323653} :answer-field/id #uuid "983fdf02-ba37-59c7-a9b5-d95bbab2e36d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92e32107-c539-550f-bff6-25f3bfd09c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-39324" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot k+6\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-7)+(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-8k+12+14-10 \\\\  & =-8k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8k+16 & =0 \\\\ 8k & =16 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82672cb0-6564-54ce-8694-7fca64c06cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131247} {:answer/id #uuid "85402023-1bba-59b8-aa36-24a9c349fe90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131248} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa7f4b4-a8ab-5ccf-bf74-146546134f1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131249} {:answer/id #uuid "38bbe9ae-51ac-52c9-b93b-b8ca5d1c3cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131250} {:answer/id #uuid "63db46b9-15e9-5e25-9188-bb63792946c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63db46b9-15e9-5e25-9188-bb63792946c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131251} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2aa07e2-e28d-5d3e-9c1b-59ec45da4625" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "224c287d-59c6-53ff-b3a7-effa4f74d7b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39333" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w) & =v\\cdot u+w\\cdot u+v\\cdot u+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+w\\cdot u+0+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =w\\cdot u+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & +(2\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-9-4+5+4)+(-6+8+0+2) \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f32bf3-db88-55c8-9663-26cb47c58ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131259} {:answer/id #uuid "d483e405-2656-52f7-a66d-b20e6ec09df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131260} {:answer/id #uuid "e506fa0b-5e26-5221-9203-6fa72ce9910c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131261} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdff0db-cb85-5541-944a-9009548a6fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131262} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1be6ce-246c-5c12-9645-30e2c7f159f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e506fa0b-5e26-5221-9203-6fa72ce9910c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131261} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf56cc2-81bf-5fc5-8656-544c880ff619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c212d94f-4084-5eee-99e8-30c7436e1711" :question/math-academy-id "q-39337" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 3 \\\\ b \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-5)+b\\cdot 3+(-5)\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ 2a-15+3b+20 & =0 \\\\ 2a+3b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+b\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ -4a-6+5b-15 & =0 \\\\ -4a+5b & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-5 \\\\ -4a+5b=21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n11b=11\\,⟹\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3(1) & =-5 \\\\ 2a+3 & =-5 \\\\ 2a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-4+1=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2b050f2-162e-5426-ae50-cfcfa4ed67e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323658} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5146ae-08d9-5498-b8f7-150056b0d92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323659} {:answer/id #uuid "52cc3309-fcbd-5f55-9ebc-f0fdf1defd55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323660} {:answer/id #uuid "07d35ab7-3902-5cca-a90a-47a11a44e824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323661} {:answer/id #uuid "35319915-d299-5648-a2d7-606689197a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07d35ab7-3902-5cca-a90a-47a11a44e824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323661} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2625d1a-d4dc-5270-9f4e-bb30b28f6167" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3b5ee57-42d6-5ec3-a568-2cbc9d550a16" :question/math-academy-id "q-39914" :question/problem "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2+3-4-3 \\\\  & =-2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5-8+3 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 3+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =1+2+12-15 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is $w$ and $x$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b22576c-4852-59f7-b576-f7c35f935b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323665} {:answer/id #uuid "b0aa2eaa-8921-559a-a5d8-013c2e673062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323666} {:answer/id #uuid "af5052e3-396d-59df-bae0-a31cbab91f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323667} {:answer/id #uuid "ed757a9e-d2a8-54de-854a-4f3828047f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323668} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9dc96a-2577-5d0c-b850-80934c791d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af5052e3-396d-59df-bae0-a31cbab91f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323667} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfe8be23-0f85-5291-b431-766a7b21e14d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb8b8f76-e901-5830-b1da-cc88f2a31e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-39988" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -k\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ k \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that  $u⊥v.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =k\\cdot k+(-5)\\cdot k+(-1)\\cdot 3+(-k)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-5k-3+3k \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-2k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}-2k-3 & =0 \\\\ (k-3)(k+1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7904e4c4-5737-5f46-81c0-f68c7dc41dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323672} {:answer/id #uuid "35d0a3d6-0c42-50b7-a7bd-79fb99b93842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323673} {:answer/id #uuid "d3203c1b-6261-5200-b9f9-73b80e08cace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323674} {:answer/id #uuid "07432d94-fcc0-5186-870b-5f7480bcdca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323675} {:answer/id #uuid "a77fe866-bba7-51ac-880b-2b3f07daee91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d0a3d6-0c42-50b7-a7bd-79fb99b93842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323673} :answer-field/id #uuid "be3aa799-ba16-5cd6-925c-8d85b51927d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9a92f4f-4161-5159-b146-a1cb45d4ca87" :question/math-academy-id "q-39993" :question/problem "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-7)+8\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5-7+16-15 \\\\  & =-1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-5)+8\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15-5-8-6 \\\\  & =-4.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-5-1+8-12 \\\\  & =-10.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is 'None of the listed'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd23190-21f8-5764-a64a-ae8b56cf2afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131505} {:answer/id #uuid "915bd9ea-5ae9-5bf4-a1b4-3fb7163a34b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131506} {:answer/id #uuid "9da95282-6a5c-5b2a-a1b4-2c8bab286e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{95}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131507} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9db635-aac2-5fdd-b710-5b2cda253e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{47}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131508} {:answer/id #uuid "2643d333-480b-581a-82f7-c8d1f9d1fcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2643d333-480b-581a-82f7-c8d1f9d1fcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131509} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d8c2ce6-d4cd-5692-b876-d2489eaee463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69f9e8f7-7103-5d3a-9398-65c91081214b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40024" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 3+a\\cdot 4+b\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ -9+4a+b-4 & =0 \\\\ 4a+b & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 1+b\\cdot (-3)-4\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ -3+a-3b+16 & =0 \\\\ a-3b & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4a+b=13 \\\\ a-3b=-13\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13a=26\\,⟹\\,a=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)+b & =13 \\\\ 8+b & =13 \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=2+5=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "addcba50-8d8f-5f9b-bf3e-e3a3d230929e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323679} {:answer/id #uuid "f18315c4-cc7c-5c58-8bb9-b91c029f2181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323680} {:answer/id #uuid "da55f710-773e-5ec4-95f1-35e5b2bd9307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323681} {:answer/id #uuid "3285e49c-ebb7-5dec-88de-8f9b57bf04e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323682} {:answer/id #uuid "b85facc2-1922-5508-8ad9-a71fe8a54107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b85facc2-1922-5508-8ad9-a71fe8a54107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323683} :answer-field/id #uuid "1316463f-463c-58ac-bba9-501f4734a098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bba5886f-2418-59b8-9a3e-79459f4614a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40026" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ b+1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot b+a\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ b-3a-6-8 & =0 \\\\ b-3a & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 5+a\\cdot 6+3\\cdot (b+1)+2\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 5+6a+3b+3+10 & =0 \\\\ 6a+3b & =-18 \\\\ 2a+b & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3a+b=14 \\\\ 2a+b=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-1$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n5a=-20\\,⟹\\,a=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(-4)+b & =14 \\\\ 12+b & =14 \\\\ b & =14-12 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-4+2=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d82ab3c0-bb79-54c2-b20f-9969aa60aefc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323686} {:answer/id #uuid "65a6754d-371a-5dbf-ac79-5caddc9133ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323687} {:answer/id #uuid "5d89e50b-5d96-5d5c-8dc0-3ba2eca31669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323688} {:answer/id #uuid "e6098f24-2bf9-50ea-ace1-188a550e96f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323689} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd8a872-1a29-5029-8cfc-c4472d662b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d89e50b-5d96-5d5c-8dc0-3ba2eca31669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323688} :answer-field/id #uuid "61c8721d-fd6e-5b63-ae8e-e9576af85722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e074f30f-94a3-5b2f-8e74-268109d97f83" :question/math-academy-id "q-74077" :question/problem "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =0-4-6+10 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0-2+0+2 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0-1-9-4 \\\\  & =-14.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is $v$ and $w$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "925f125d-3206-58d8-b49a-8c84d38a8278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323693} {:answer/id #uuid "40be2118-26e0-5ddc-bfc8-97375332cc7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323694} {:answer/id #uuid "842e783d-1def-50e0-bcf3-c89b323478c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323695} {:answer/id #uuid "85f1861f-825c-5b59-a774-1f501723278c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323696} {:answer/id #uuid "05cc7d79-7a2a-5189-911f-0fcd4408adb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05cc7d79-7a2a-5189-911f-0fcd4408adb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323697} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5b43911-7915-5825-89fe-eda5345a298d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b895bae0-a22e-5a6a-874e-7b72daf60f3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74084" :question/problem "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =4+0+0-4 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-8-1+0+3 \\\\  & =-6.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0-3+6-3 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is $v$ and $x$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9b8d22-5989-52a1-8726-d648219bbec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323700} {:answer/id #uuid "af60fc1f-15df-5687-b3e1-04a9f3ae1539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323701} {:answer/id #uuid "36830d78-8c3e-5983-9655-f98090bb4d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323702} {:answer/id #uuid "a01b0146-c485-5aa7-bdcf-dfcfba4fff5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323703} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b71968-f0e7-5eb1-9b2f-7d51c86afa4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36830d78-8c3e-5983-9655-f98090bb4d77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323702} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eadda4f-9a15-5de7-aeea-9be38091ec6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96a85d0f-f840-5482-ab7d-823e5b207944" :question/math-academy-id "q-74089" :question/problem "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4-1+0+5 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-4-3+5 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+0-6-5 \\\\  & =-7.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is $v$ and $w$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0354fce-c0d0-5f3d-b831-ebd51a36073b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323707} {:answer/id #uuid "58ef4eba-2160-5a0c-b754-f8551b22e411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323708} {:answer/id #uuid "fb847907-0109-5d80-b488-8df6a075ccd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323709} {:answer/id #uuid "77fa8a92-4a52-5afc-9a58-c9dcdfb86197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323710} {:answer/id #uuid "569b455e-96ae-5433-89e7-3341ece673e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "569b455e-96ae-5433-89e7-3341ece673e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323711} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b793489-2acd-5e5b-9a06-ae9fed697ed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4a6e848-c5de-56c0-ae5b-e5f727f066f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74092" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k+(-3)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-4)+(-5)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2k-9-4+5 \\\\  & =2k-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432fda16-cf5e-5024-beee-34714bb97e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323714} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff0e573-ae02-569b-90a5-98b2c893333d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323715} {:answer/id #uuid "52e73195-a543-56df-a1e9-dfa3077e7fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323716} {:answer/id #uuid "9059c385-9e12-549e-955f-f7b0badc7ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323717} {:answer/id #uuid "c0bc277c-bbee-5e3a-8943-0eef7326ef5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52e73195-a543-56df-a1e9-dfa3077e7fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323716} :answer-field/id #uuid "a316dc4f-fcbb-50d6-9d40-508f70bcd4a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2350a5d-3530-57c1-8b04-eddbcad053e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-74093" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $a⊥b.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ and $b$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2}+{a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3}+{a}_{4}\\cdot {b}_{4} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-3)+k\\cdot k \\\\  & =2k+3-3+{k}^{2} \\\\  & =2k+{k}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+{k}^{2} & =0 \\\\ k(2+k) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df3f5af7-1a43-5619-b6e5-5afc8eebe51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323721} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d74241-bf94-59c0-8dee-9547d62ddabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323722} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8bed27-0d40-54c5-83f9-c69c80927e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323723} {:answer/id #uuid "86979ed9-b31d-5f4c-b9a2-cd2130c295fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323724} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5ecf5a-2fd1-5153-9849-e18c6bcd8ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df3f5af7-1a43-5619-b6e5-5afc8eebe51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323721} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b466534-924f-51b5-9206-b226dfcc578c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1f7ed5f-b3a3-5824-9338-a684659b92e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74180" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u-2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+2w).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+2w) & =u\\cdot w-(2v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot (2w) \\\\  & =u\\cdot w-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v+2(u\\cdot w) \\\\  & =0-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =-2(v\\cdot w)+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =-2(2\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +(2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-2(-4+0+0-1)+(4+0-6-5) \\\\  & =10-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69e5bfa3-21b6-5514-9b0c-1c30fbcf1a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323728} {:answer/id #uuid "e95446f7-4be9-5905-9fed-969562662944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323729} {:answer/id #uuid "80f78e2e-858f-5f4f-9641-d20e2c7d5b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323730} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ebc4f7-0b0e-5b95-a283-0a52c898f36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323731} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdb5a21-4674-5af3-9c62-0777a73343be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0ebc4f7-0b0e-5b95-a283-0a52c898f36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323731} :answer-field/id #uuid "469c5318-397a-5add-b681-9ea48166156d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df5ce87c-4049-5f98-86f3-21fa4e066b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74181" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal. Find $(2u+3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot (v-w).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u+3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot (v-w) & =(2u)\\cdot v+(3w)\\cdot v+u\\cdot v-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =2(u\\cdot v)+3(w\\cdot v)+u\\cdot v-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+3(w\\cdot v)+0-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =3(w\\cdot v)-u\\cdot w \\\\  & =3(0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & -(0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =3(0+2+0+-2)-(0-1-9-4) \\\\  & =0-(-14) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66457cb8-ec90-594b-80be-b083595d301e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323735} {:answer/id #uuid "950e9dda-a450-5588-b8ba-d65031016ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323736} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2ac2da-706c-53ee-981a-d4ec4aedcbae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323737} {:answer/id #uuid "58757990-c15e-5143-ab90-7247e4d172f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323738} {:answer/id #uuid "309d4fda-f423-5c7c-acdd-e8ceab96267c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c2ac2da-706c-53ee-981a-d4ec4aedcbae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323737} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc43d54-3467-56e3-b1b8-e84e8a0ebb97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47e230b4-36cd-5480-a2a4-06667e6b2955" :question/math-academy-id "q-74192" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-2)+a\\cdot 1+b\\cdot 5+5\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ -4+a+5b-10 & =0 \\\\ a+5b & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 4+b\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 2+4a-b+5 & =0 \\\\ 4a-b & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}a+5b=14 \\\\ 4a-b=-7\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-4$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-21b=-63\\,⟹\\,b=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+5(3) & =14 \\\\ a+15 & =14 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=-1-3=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27852ccd-8631-5f16-b4ac-1dbbd4b18b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323742} {:answer/id #uuid "2a51c2f8-f406-52ce-894a-c35794dd12a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323743} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d6032f-9bb5-5975-9d47-64bb15098761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323744} {:answer/id #uuid "dee88e56-7735-5124-bbec-e71d1e575f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323745} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdecc2f-5846-5705-9b1f-72ced2be3fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dee88e56-7735-5124-bbec-e71d1e575f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323745} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf62017-d86b-5fdb-b047-cf784dcab5d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d69dffe-6275-5f8b-9994-a10185340e9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-74195" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+b\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ a+0+b-2 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1+b\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ -a+0+2b+5 & =0 \\\\ -a+2b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a+b=2 \\\\ -a+2b=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the first equation to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3b=-3\\,⟹\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+(-1) & =2 \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=3-(-1)=4.\n$$"}]}
